Port the rear camera from the phone's own vendor file

The IMX682 driver did not come from this phone: its tables were copied from
MediaTek's GPL driver for the same sensor. That left FOUR things wrong, all of
them checkable against com.qti.sensormodule.j20c_*_imx682_wide.bin, which is
what the vendor Android hands to CamX:

                     vendor           driver             where it shows
    input clock      19.200.000 Hz    24.000.000         powerSetting
    lanes            3                2                  laneAssign = 0x210
                                                         and 0x0114 = 0x02
    link_freq        435,2 MHz        1020 MHz           output PLL
    tables           from this phone  from a MediaTek    regSetting

THE CLOCK, WHICH IS THE MOST TREACHEROUS ONE
--------------------------------------------
Changing assigned-clock-rates is not enough. The sensor itself carries the clock
it BELIEVES it has in 0x0136/0x0137, in 8.8 fixed point, and the inherited table
left it at 0x1800 = 24 MHz. With that value wrong, all the PLL multipliers
produce the wrong frequencies even if the physical clock is the right one. It
becomes 0x1333.

THE MODE
--------
The file carries FIVE modes, all of them at three lanes:

    9248x6944  1x1  hts=20576 vts=7030  link 379,2 MHz
    4624x3472  2x2  hts= 9432 vts=3528  link 476,8 MHz
    3840x2160  2x2  hts= 5144 vts=2218  link 435,2 MHz
    2312x1304  4x4  hts= 2728 vts=1680  link 435,2 MHz   <- this one
    1920x1080  4x4  hts= 2728 vts=1148  link 520,0 MHz

Only the 2312x1304 one is declared, and on purpose. With the front camera it was
learned that libcamera ALWAYS takes the biggest mode and that Bayer development
runs on the CPU: with the 20 MP one declared the viewfinder ran at 53 ms per
frame and darker on top of that. This one bins 4x4, so it is 3 MP instead of 64
and sixteen times more light per pixel. The other four are noted down in the
comment.

HOW IT WAS EXTRACTED
--------------------
With camera/extract-rear.py. The format was not obvious: regSetting does NOT
carry (address, value) pairs but groups of ten u64 of which only the third is
useful -the address-, and the values live apart in 8-byte registerData
descriptors, in the same order. Paired that way you get 0x0112/3 = 0x0a0a
(RAW10) and 0x0114 = 0x02; any other way you get garbage.
--- a/drivers/media/i2c/imx682.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/imx682.c
@@ -74,15 +74,15 @@
  * aborts with "Pixel clock is too high for CSIPHY". See the comment on the
  * mode table.
  */
-#define IMX682_LINK_FREQ		1020000000LL
-#define IMX682_DATA_LANES		2
+#define IMX682_LINK_FREQ		435200000LL
+#define IMX682_DATA_LANES		3
 #define IMX682_BITS_PER_PIXEL		10
 
 /* pixels per second coming out of the link, which is what V4L2 expects */
 #define IMX682_PIXEL_RATE \
 	(IMX682_LINK_FREQ * 2 * IMX682_DATA_LANES / IMX682_BITS_PER_PIXEL)
 
-#define IMX682_XVCLK_HZ			24000000
+#define IMX682_XVCLK_HZ			19200000
 
